course: dictionary
lesson: 12001
title: Charades: a game and a pretence
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Charades is a word with two meanings, and the sentence around it tells you which one to use. Look for clues: words like party, guess and act point to the game, while words like meeting, promise and fake point to the pretence.
explain: «We played charades at Gran's house on Christmas Day.» The clue is played, which tells you charades is a game. Check it by swapping in "a game": "We played a game at Gran's house" still makes sense.
explain: «The meeting was a charade; nobody had any real plans.» The clue is nobody had any real plans. Here a charade is a silly pretence that everyone can see through, not a game.
fact: Charades is a noun. The game is written as charades, with an s, and it takes a singular verb: «Charades is my favourite game.»
fact: In the game, the actor does not speak. They use signs, such as holding up fingers to show how many words there are.
fact: Tugging an ear is a signal for "sounds like", which helps players guess a word that sounds like another word.
fact: «A charade» means a silly pretence that everyone can see through.
fact: Look at the words around the word: «the meeting was a charade» tells you the pretence is about something serious that is not real.
exercise: «We played charades at Gran's house on Christmas Day.» What kind of thing is charades here? | A silly pretence that everyone can see through | *A party game with acting and no talking | A song about snow | A type of Christmas dinner
exercise: «Dad acted out a film title without a single word, and we guessed it.» Which word shows this is the game? | title | *guessed | film | Dad
exercise: «The promise to fix the roads was a charade; nothing was ever done.» What does charade mean here? | A long walk | *A silly pretence that everyone can see through | A fun party game | A sweet treat
test: Ella sees her brother hold up three fingers and tug his ear. Which game are they playing? | Hide and seek | *Charades, guessing a three-word phrase that sounds like something | Snap | Tag
test: «The sale was a charade: the shop had put the prices up first.» What does this tell you? | The shop was running a fun party game | The prices were very low | *The sale was fake and only for show | The shop was closed for the day
exam: Why does «the meeting was a charade» mean a pretence rather than a game? | *Because nobody was really trying, and everyone could see it was fake | Because the meeting was held in a park | Because everyone was playing charades | Because the meeting had lots of children in it
